{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"9f4740c5-99e4-4463-bce8-a391c1ab3ef1","status":"SUCCESS","response":"I have identified and resolved the issue causing the heart rate corridors to be clamped to the watch's zones.\n\n### Summary of Changes\n\n1. **Fixed Target Range Key in [`gen.py`](file:///private/tmp/h-35RIwT/gen.py):**\n   In `hr_target(step)`, changed `\"value\": lo` to `\"start\": lo`, returning `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}`.\n\n2. **Documented in [`ANSWER.md`](file:///private/tmp/h-35RIwT/ANSWER.md):**\n   Detailed what changed and the root cause of the issue:\n   - In Intervals.icu's `workout_doc` schema, a range corridor must be specified using `start` and `end`.\n   - When `\"value\"` was provided alongside `\"end\"`, Intervals.icu treated the step as having a single target at `value` (`lo`), ignoring `end`.\n   - When exporting single-value targets to Garmin Connect / Garmin FIT format, Intervals.icu maps the target to the corresponding preset zone index (`target_value = zone_num`), causing the watch to display that entire preset zone's boundaries instead of a custom target corridor.\n   - Specifying both `start` and `end` prompts Intervals.icu to export custom range bounds (`target_value = 0` with custom low/high limits), displaying the planned corridors as intended.\n","duration_seconds":716.873012,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":348342,"output_tokens":4620,"thinking_tokens":0,"cache_read_tokens":6806430,"total_tokens":352962}}}
